FINAL IVX RORK REMOVAL CLOSEOUT
REMOVE RORK 100% FROM CODE, INFRASTRUCTURE, ACCESS, BUILDS, DEPLOYMENT, AND RUNTIME
EXECUTE SAFELY — BACK UP FIRST — VERIFY EVERYTHING — THEN REVOKE

OBJECTIVE

Complete the final cleanup required to make IVX Holdings fully independent from Rork.

This task is not limited to removing an SDK or deleting branding.

The final state must prove that IVX can be:

- developed;
- built;
- tested;
- deployed;
- updated;
- monitored;
- restored;
- operated;
- and transferred

without any Rork account, Rork SDK, Rork domain, Rork deployment path, Rork credential, Rork workspace, Rork signing identity, or Rork-controlled infrastructure.

Do not delete or revoke access before backups and owner-controlled replacement paths are verified.

==================================================
PHASE 1 — CREATE THE FINAL MIGRATION SNAPSHOT
==================================================

Before removing anything:

1. Export the complete canonical GitHub repository.
2. Export all active branches and tags.
3. Export the current Render configuration.
4. Export Supabase schema and migrations.
5. Export a verified database backup.
6. Export storage bucket inventory.
7. Export Expo/EAS project configuration.
8. Export Android signing metadata without exposing secrets.
9. Export iOS signing and bundle configuration metadata.
10. Export DNS records.
11. Export environment-variable names and ownership.
12. Export GitHub Actions workflows and secrets inventory.
13. Export deployment hooks.
14. Export webhooks.
15. Export current production health and commit proof.
16. Record the rollback commit and last stable APK/AAB.
17. Record the current live backend deployment.
18. Store all evidence in an owner-controlled migration folder.

Return:

- snapshot timestamp;
- repository SHA;
- database backup ID;
- APK/AAB checksum;
- active deployment ID;
- rollback instructions.

==================================================
PHASE 2 — CONFIRM THE CANONICAL OWNER-CONTROLLED SYSTEM
==================================================

Confirm one canonical production source:

Repository:
ibb142/rork-global-real-estate-invest

Verify:

- the repository is owned by the owner;
- the default branch is correct;
- production builds from this repository;
- no hidden Rork mirror is authoritative;
- no Rork proxy repository remains between GitHub and Render;
- every production service points directly to the owner repository;
- branch protections exist;
- owner recovery access exists;
- current GitHub HEAD is recorded.

List and classify all other repositories:

ACTIVE
ARCHIVE
MIGRATE
DELETE_LATER
UNKNOWN

Do not delete old repositories until production independence is proven.

==================================================
PHASE 3 — REMOVE ALL RORK CODE DEPENDENCIES
==================================================

Search the entire codebase, lockfiles, native projects, build scripts, generated configuration, and CI for:

- rork
- @rork-ai
- rork.com
- rork.app
- rork-direct.workers.dev
- RORK_
- EXPO_PUBLIC_RORK_
- withRorkMetro
- Rork toolkit
- Rork runtime
- Rork project IDs
- Rork bundle IDs
- Rork deployment routes
- Rork webhooks
- Rork analytics
- Rork telemetry
- Rork auth
- Rork storage
- Rork proxy
- Rork-generated build scripts
- Rork branding

For every match provide:

- file;
- line;
- purpose;
- runtime impact;
- action taken;
- replacement;
- test evidence.

Remove:

- Rork SDK packages;
- Rork imports;
- Rork plugins;
- Rork Metro wrappers;
- Rork environment reads;
- Rork URLs;
- Rork headers;
- Rork fallback providers;
- Rork-specific feature flags;
- Rork build hooks;
- Rork banners and branding.

After removal:

- regenerate lockfiles;
- run dependency install from a clean state;
- confirm no Rork package is installed directly or transitively;
- confirm the app builds without Rork.

==================================================
PHASE 4 — REPLACE ALL RORK DEPLOYMENT PATHS
==================================================

Audit:

- render.yaml;
- Render service repository bindings;
- Render deploy hooks;
- GitHub Apps;
- GitHub webhooks;
- Cloudflare Workers;
- Vercel projects;
- Docker registries;
- CI pipelines;
- Expo update channels;
- EAS build profiles.

Required final flow:

Owner GitHub
→ Owner CI
→ Owner Build
→ Owner Render / AWS / EAS
→ IVX Production

Remove any path like:

Owner GitHub
→ Rork proxy/router
→ Render

For every production service verify:

- direct repository URL;
- branch;
- build command;
- start command;
- environment;
- deployment hook owner;
- current commit;
- rollback method.

Regenerate all deployment hooks under owner-controlled accounts.

==================================================
PHASE 5 — REMOVE RORK FROM GITHUB
==================================================

In every repository and organization:

1. Remove Rork collaborators.
2. Remove Rork teams.
3. Remove Rork GitHub Apps.
4. Remove Rork OAuth Apps.
5. Remove Rork deploy keys.
6. Remove unknown machine users.
7. Remove Rork webhooks.
8. Revoke Rork personal access tokens where visible.
9. Review Actions secrets.
10. Review environment protections.
11. Review branch bypass permissions.
12. Review repository transfer permissions.
13. Review organization owners.
14. Review audit logs.

Do not remove access until owner push, CI, and deployment are confirmed.

Return before/after access matrix.

==================================================
PHASE 6 — REMOVE RORK FROM RENDER
==================================================

Audit every Render account, team, service, environment, deploy hook, and repository binding.

Verify:

- account owner;
- billing owner;
- team members;
- service owner;
- Git provider;
- repository;
- branch;
- deploy hook;
- environment variables;
- service tokens;
- API keys;
- custom domains.

Remove:

- Rork users;
- Rork teams;
- Rork service tokens;
- Rork deploy hooks;
- Rork GitHub connections;
- Rork repository proxies;
- unknown collaborators.

Regenerate owner-controlled:

- deploy hooks;
- service API keys;
- CI connection;
- rollback workflow.

Deploy one harmless version marker from the owner repository and prove it reaches production without Rork.

==================================================
PHASE 7 — REMOVE RORK FROM SUPABASE
==================================================

Audit:

- organization members;
- project members;
- database users;
- service-role use;
- access tokens;
- edge-function secrets;
- API keys;
- storage policies;
- database webhooks;
- Realtime channels;
- external integrations;
- scheduled jobs.

Remove Rork access after backups are verified.

Rotate where Rork may have had visibility:

- service-role key;
- anon key only if necessary and migration-safe;
- database password;
- access tokens;
- webhook secrets;
- function secrets.

Update all owner-controlled services with rotated credentials.

Verify:

- authentication works;
- database queries work;
- storage works;
- Realtime works;
- RLS works;
- backend works;
- mobile works.

Do not expose secret values in the report.

==================================================
PHASE 8 — REMOVE RORK FROM EXPO AND EAS
==================================================

Audit:

- Expo owner;
- organization;
- project ID;
- slug;
- update URL;
- runtime version;
- build profiles;
- channels;
- credentials;
- environment variables;
- webhooks;
- collaborators;
- access tokens.

Remove:

- Rork collaborators;
- Rork environment variables;
- Rork project references;
- Rork update channels;
- Rork hooks;
- Rork organization ownership.

Verify the IVX Expo/EAS project belongs to the owner.

Build:

- development build;
- production APK;
- production AAB;
- iOS build when credentials are available.

Prove the app can receive updates from the owner-controlled Expo project only.

==================================================
PHASE 9 — ANDROID OWNERSHIP CLEANUP
==================================================

Verify:

- Android application ID belongs to IVX;
- package name is final;
- signing key is owner-controlled;
- keystore backup exists;
- Play Console ownership is owner-controlled;
- service account is owner-controlled;
- Firebase project is owner-controlled if used;
- push credentials are owner-controlled;
- deep links do not reference Rork;
- update URLs do not reference Rork;
- native configuration contains no Rork IDs.

Rebuild after all removals.

Return:

- application ID;
- version name;
- version code;
- artifact ID;
- checksum;
- source commit;
- build owner.

==================================================
PHASE 10 — IOS OWNERSHIP CLEANUP
==================================================

Verify and correct:

- bundle identifiers;
- Apple Team ID;
- App Store Connect ownership;
- certificates;
- provisioning profiles;
- push keys;
- associated domains;
- URL schemes;
- entitlements;
- Expo/EAS Apple credentials.

All Rork-owned bundle IDs must be removed.

Final IVX bundle IDs must be owner-controlled and registered in the owner’s Apple account.

Rebuild and submit to TestFlight when credentials are available.

Do not mark iOS independent until a successful owner-controlled build exists.

==================================================
PHASE 11 — REMOVE RORK FROM AWS, DNS, AND CDN
==================================================

Audit:

- AWS root account;
- IAM users;
- IAM roles;
- access keys;
- Route 53;
- S3;
- CloudFront;
- EC2;
- certificates;
- logs;
- Lambda;
- queues;
- secret stores.

Remove:

- Rork IAM identities;
- Rork roles;
- Rork keys;
- Rork trust policies;
- Rork deployment users.

Audit every IVX domain:

- ivxholding.com;
- chat.ivxholding.com;
- api.ivxholding.com;
- all subdomains.

Verify:

- registrar owner;
- DNS owner;
- record destination;
- SSL certificate;
- expiration;
- no Rork proxy;
- no Rork redirect;
- no Rork nameserver;
- no Rork-controlled certificate.

==================================================
PHASE 12 — ROTATE ALL POTENTIALLY EXPOSED CREDENTIALS
==================================================

After replacement paths are proven, rotate all credentials that Rork may have accessed.

Examples:

- GitHub tokens;
- Render API keys;
- Render deploy hooks;
- Supabase service credentials;
- database passwords;
- Expo tokens;
- EAS credentials;
- AWS keys;
- webhook secrets;
- email credentials;
- SMS credentials;
- AI-provider keys;
- payment-provider keys;
- analytics tokens;
- crash-reporting keys.

For each credential record only:

- variable name;
- service;
- environment;
- rotation status;
- updated services;
- verification result.

Never print secret values.

==================================================
PHASE 13 — REMOVE RORK FROM IVX IA AND AUTONOMOUS WORKERS
==================================================

Prove IVX IA can operate without Rork’s interface or sandbox.

Required owner-controlled paths:

- IVX native app;
- IVX web chat;
- owner dashboard;
- documented backend API;
- autonomous dashboard.

Audit:

- model provider;
- tool bindings;
- job queue;
- worker runtime;
- repository access;
- deployment access;
- database access;
- proof ledger;
- prompt templates;
- environment variables;
- Rork fallback routes.

Remove all Rork fallback execution paths.

A developer request submitted inside IVX must create and execute a job without opening Rork.

If any execution still requires the Rork sandbox, mark:

RORK_RUNTIME_DEPENDENCY_REMAINS

Do not claim 100% independence until this is eliminated.

==================================================
PHASE 14 — NETWORK-LEVEL ZERO-RORK VERIFICATION
==================================================

Run production network capture for:

- mobile launch;
- login;
- chat;
- reels;
- portfolio;
- CRM;
- investor pipeline;
- file upload;
- autonomous job;
- backend health;
- deployment callback.

Verify zero requests to:

- rork.com;
- rork.app;
- rork-direct.workers.dev;
- any discovered Rork domain;
- any Rork proxy IP or hostname.

Do not rely only on source-code grep.

Use:

- mobile network logs;
- backend outbound request logs;
- DNS logs;
- proxy/CDN logs;
- runtime telemetry.

Return a complete list of external domains contacted.

Required result:

RORK PRODUCTION NETWORK REQUESTS: 0

==================================================
PHASE 15 — CLEAN BUILD OUTSIDE RORK
==================================================

From a clean owner-controlled environment:

1. Clone the canonical GitHub repository.
2. Install dependencies.
3. Run lint.
4. Run typecheck.
5. Run unit tests.
6. Run integration tests.
7. Build backend.
8. Start backend.
9. Build web.
10. Build Android APK.
11. Build Android AAB.
12. Build iOS when credentials are available.
13. Run smoke tests.
14. Verify owner login.
15. Verify chat.
16. Verify critical APIs.

No Rork browser, Rork workspace, Rork SDK, or Rork proxy may be used.

==================================================
PHASE 16 — DISASTER-RECOVERY TEST
==================================================

Prove the owner can recover IVX without Rork.

Run an isolated recovery:

- clean repository clone;
- restore environment configuration;
- restore database backup;
- restore storage inventory;
- start backend;
- build mobile;
- verify authentication;
- verify critical data;
- verify IVX IA;
- verify deployment.

Record:

- recovery duration;
- dependencies;
- blockers;
- recovery target;
- proof.

==================================================
PHASE 17 — FINAL REGRESSION
==================================================

After all cleanup:

Test:

- landing page;
- owner login;
- members;
- investors;
- buyers;
- CRM;
- capital pipeline;
- portfolio;
- wallet;
- transactions;
- reels;
- chat;
- uploads;
- notifications;
- Executive Layer;
- Autonomous Dashboard;
- IVX IA Senior Developer;
- Android;
- iOS;
- web;
- backend;
- database;
- storage;
- deployments.

Fix every regression before final revocation.

==================================================
PHASE 18 — FINAL ACCESS REVOCATION
==================================================

Only after all previous phases pass:

- revoke Rork GitHub access;
- revoke Rork Render access;
- revoke Rork Supabase access;
- revoke Rork Expo/EAS access;
- revoke Rork AWS access;
- revoke Rork Apple access;
- revoke Rork Google Play access;
- remove Rork webhooks;
- delete Rork deployment hooks;
- invalidate Rork tokens;
- remove Rork service accounts;
- archive or delete the Rork workspace only after evidence is exported.

Record every revocation with timestamp and owner confirmation.

==================================================
PHASE 19 — REQUIRED FINAL EVIDENCE
==================================================

Return:

CODE
- all Rork references found;
- all removed references;
- remaining references with reason;
- packages removed;
- files changed;
- tests.

OWNERSHIP
- GitHub access matrix;
- Render access matrix;
- Supabase access matrix;
- Expo/EAS access matrix;
- AWS access matrix;
- Apple access matrix;
- Google Play access matrix;
- DNS ownership.

BUILDS
- backend build;
- web build;
- APK;
- AAB;
- iOS build if available;
- checksums;
- source commit.

DEPLOYMENT
- GitHub HEAD;
- Render commit;
- runtime commit;
- APK source commit;
- EAS build commit;
- commit parity.

RUNTIME
- production external-domain list;
- Rork network calls;
- health checks;
- critical workflow results.

SECURITY
- credentials rotated;
- deploy hooks regenerated;
- unknown access removed;
- audit log evidence.

RECOVERY
- backup verification;
- restore test;
- recovery duration.

==================================================
FINAL ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
==================================================

Do not mark IVX 100% Rork-independent until:

[ ] No Rork SDK is installed.
[ ] No Rork import exists.
[ ] No Rork runtime URL exists.
[ ] No production service uses a Rork repository proxy.
[ ] No Render deployment depends on Rork.
[ ] No GitHub App, collaborator, key, or webhook gives Rork access.
[ ] No Supabase member, token, key, or integration gives Rork access.
[ ] No Expo/EAS project or update channel is controlled by Rork.
[ ] Android identifiers and signing are owner-controlled.
[ ] iOS identifiers and signing are owner-controlled.
[ ] No AWS/DNS/CDN resource is controlled by Rork.
[ ] All potentially exposed credentials are rotated.
[ ] IVX IA runs without the Rork interface or sandbox.
[ ] Autonomous jobs run without Rork.
[ ] Clean builds succeed outside Rork.
[ ] Production deploy succeeds outside Rork.
[ ] Network logs show zero Rork requests.
[ ] Disaster recovery succeeds without Rork.
[ ] Full regression passes.
[ ] Owner controls every production account.
[ ] Rork access is revoked everywhere.
[ ] GitHub HEAD equals production runtime and release artifacts.

==================================================
FINAL RESPONSE FORMAT
==================================================

OVERALL STATUS:
PASS / PARTIAL / FAIL

RORK CODE DEPENDENCIES:
FOUND:
REMOVED:
REMAINING:

RORK ACCOUNT ACCESS:
GITHUB:
RENDER:
SUPABASE:
EXPO/EAS:
AWS:
APPLE:
GOOGLE PLAY:
DNS:

CREDENTIAL ROTATION:
COMPLETE / PARTIAL / NOT STARTED

BUILDS WITHOUT RORK:
BACKEND:
